WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:03.820
He was one of the most influential and talented
entertainers the world has ever seen.

00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:08.560
But he was also one of the most complex – generous
and loyal friend, champion of the underdog,

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:13.780
devoted family man, womanizer and volatile,
loud-mouthed tough guy.

00:00:13.780 --> 00:00:17.510
He lived a life on the edge – full of excitement,
danger and passion.

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:22.390
Sow, just how did the skinny Italian kid from
New Jersey became an international superstar

00:00:22.390 --> 00:00:25.840
and the world’s first true multi-media artist?

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:31.160
In this week’s Biographics we go behind
the veneer to get up close with the Chairman

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.130
of the Board.

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:39.710
Early Years

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:47.010
Francis Albert Sinatra was born on December
12th, 1915 in Hoboken, New Jersey.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:50.739
He was the only child of Italian immigrants,
Martin and Dolly Sinatra.

00:00:50.739 --> 00:00:54.570
As such he was pampered by his parents, who
lavished the best of what they could afford

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:55.570
on their son.

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:58.329
Still, little Frankie grew up lonely and alone.

00:00:58.329 --> 00:01:02.050
Martin Sinatra was a down to earth, easy going
and hard- working City Fireman.

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:06.310
His mother was just the opposite, driven,
involved in political issues and proving herself

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:08.350
the driving force of the family.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:14.050
She belonged to every organization in Hoboken
and had a reputation as an extremely dominating

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:15.050
character.

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:17.920
While both of his parents worked, young Frank
learned to look after himself.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:21.720
If he wasn’t home alone, he’d be wandering
the neighbourhood, where he quickly discovered

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:23.230
how to become street smart.

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:26.770
He grew up in a world of prohibition, bootleggers
and Speakeasies.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:29.260
School was never a priority for the teenage
Frank.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:33.920
He would often skip class to hang out in pool
halls, boxing gyms and on street corners.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:38.930
He soon developed a reputation as a stubborn,
ornery kid who never backed down.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:43.040
Often the subject of racial taunts, he would
never let an insult pass.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:46.760
A friend recalled that once he and Frank were
walking down the street when someone said,

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:48.610
‘Hey you little wop,’ to Frank.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:52.080
His friend told him to keep on walking but
an enraged Frank blurted out,

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.040
I’m gonna’ walk all over his face!

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.320
The only problem was that Frank didn’t really
know how to fight.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:59.350
He got beaten to a pulp.

00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:01.610
When his friend asked him if it was worth
it he replied . . .

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:04.090
Hell, yeah . . . He’ll never call me a wop
again.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.680
However, two days later, the same thing happened
again – same guy, same result.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.829
By the age of sixteen, Frank had ditched school
altogether.

00:02:10.829 --> 00:02:14.599
He worked at a series of odd jobs, including
as a dockworker and newspaper boy.

00:02:14.599 --> 00:02:17.760
But he often got bored and would quite a solid
job after just a few weeks.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:22.629
When he walked out on his job working in the
refrigeration units of cargo ships, his father

00:02:22.629 --> 00:02:23.629
had had enough.

00:02:23.629 --> 00:02:26.189
He referred to his son as a ‘quitter’
and told him . . .

00:02:26.189 --> 00:02:30.200
If you want to be a bum, go somewhere else
and be a bum.

00:02:30.200 --> 00:02:31.489
Frank didn’t have to be told twice.

00:02:31.489 --> 00:02:35.719
He packed up his few belongings in a suitcase
and took the train to New York City.

00:02:35.719 --> 00:02:39.109
By now he had set his sights on making it
as a singer.

00:02:39.109 --> 00:02:41.540
But no doors opened for the skinny Italian
kid.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:44.739
He couldn’t find any employment at all,
let along a signing gig.

00:02:44.739 --> 00:02:48.389
With nowhere to stay and no money for food,
he headed home.

00:02:48.389 --> 00:02:52.390
Outwardly Dolly mocked her son’s singing
ambitions, referring to him as Mr Big-Shot

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:54.099
Singer before smacking him on the head.

00:02:54.099 --> 00:02:58.220
But behind the scenes, she began visiting
clubs and asking managers to give her boy

00:02:58.220 --> 00:02:59.220
a chance.

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.799
This resulted in a short-term gig in a Hoboken
club.

00:03:01.799 --> 00:03:05.569
But the job didn’t last – Frank got into
a fight with the proprietor and was shown

00:03:05.569 --> 00:03:06.569
the door.

00:03:06.569 --> 00:03:10.019
When he was seventeen, Dolly and Marty lent
Frank sixty-five dollars so he could buy a

00:03:10.019 --> 00:03:13.109
portable public address system and sheet music
arrangements.

00:03:13.109 --> 00:03:16.569
This put him a step ahead of other aspiring
club performers.

00:03:16.569 --> 00:03:18.890
Frank then began collecting orchestrations.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:21.409
He later explained his strategy . . .
Bands needed them.

00:03:21.409 --> 00:03:22.409
I had them.

00:03:22.409 --> 00:03:26.790
If the local orchestra wanted to use my arrangements,
and they always did because I had a large

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:30.769
and up-to-the-minute collection, they had
to take singer Sinatra too.

00:03:30.769 --> 00:03:33.890
In this way, Frank managed to establish a
foothold in the local club scene.

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:37.170
His hero was Bing Crosby, and he tried to
copy the Crosby sound.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:40.559
But when he noticed virtually every other
singer out there was doing the same thing,

00:03:40.559 --> 00:03:44.329
he made the decision to establish his own
unique sound.

00:03:44.329 --> 00:03:47.419
Frank, Nancy &amp; Success

00:03:47.419 --> 00:03:53.069
In the summer of 1934, the eighteen-year-old
Frank met seventeen-year-old Nancy Carol Barbato,

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:56.430
who came from a poor family in Jersey City,
New Jersey.

00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:00.361
Nancy was sitting on her front porch doing
her nails when Frank walked up to her with

00:04:00.361 --> 00:04:01.840
a ukulele in his hand.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.200
His opening line was . . .
Yo, what about me.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.779
I could use a manicure too!

00:04:05.779 --> 00:04:08.900
The spark was lit and the romance blossomed
over the next four years.

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:12.409
They lived one town apart and Frank would
frequently take the bus to visit and date

00:04:12.409 --> 00:04:13.599
Nancy.

00:04:13.599 --> 00:04:16.419
During these years, Frank was building his
singing experience.

00:04:16.419 --> 00:04:18.519
Most gigs would pay around two dollars a night.

00:04:18.519 --> 00:04:22.360
Sometimes he performed at roadhouses for nothing
more than a sandwich or cigarettes.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:27.419
Frank’s first break came on September 8th,
1935 when he auditioned for a spot on the

00:04:27.419 --> 00:04:31.509
NBC radio show Major Bowes and His Original
Amateur Hour.

00:04:31.509 --> 00:04:35.340
The show was broadcast live from the Capitol
Theatre in New York City.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:39.620
Frank teamed up with a group called the Three
Flashes and they became the Hoboken Four.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:44.580
They performed the Bing Crosby hit ‘Shine’
and won a spot on the show, which was a 1930’s

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:46.199
forerunner to American Idol.

00:04:46.199 --> 00:04:50.370
Listeners phoned in to vote for their favorite
act and the Hoboken Four proved so popular

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:53.310
that Major Bowes invited them to return several
times.

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:57.389
However, the radio show appearances didn’t
lead to any lasting success.

00:04:57.389 --> 00:05:02.610
Frank returned to Hoboken and soon found himself
once more begging for any jobs he could find.

00:05:02.610 --> 00:05:05.139
In 1939, Frank and Nancy were married.

00:05:05.139 --> 00:05:08.439
Not long after that Frank was singing in a
club called the Rustic Cabin.

00:05:08.439 --> 00:05:13.159
In the audience was Harry James, a big band
leader who was auditioning for a lead singer.

00:05:13.159 --> 00:05:15.889
James was impressed with Frank’s way of
talking a lyric.

00:05:15.889 --> 00:05:18.680
He invited Sinatra to audition the next day.

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:22.889
Although there were a lot of others who showed
up for the job, when Frank opened his mouth

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:24.590
the matter was settled.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:29.319
He made his debut with the Harry James Band
on June 30th, 1939 at the Hippodrome Theater

00:05:29.319 --> 00:05:30.319
in Baltimore.

00:05:30.319 --> 00:05:34.940
The rest of that summer and into the fall
he toured with the band to enthusiastic audiences.

00:05:34.940 --> 00:05:38.800
By 1940 Sinatra’s popularity was growing
steadily.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:42.780
Then he switched bands mid-stream, jumping
at the chance to sign on with the more famous

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:44.380
Tommy Dorsey Orchestra.

00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:47.000
The deal that Frank struck, though was hardly
in his favour.

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:51.689
He would give up a third of his earnings for
life, plus 10 percent for Tommy’s agent.

00:05:51.689 --> 00:05:55.949
But all that the young Frank, who had recently
become a father, wanted was to sing and be

00:05:55.949 --> 00:05:56.949
famous.

00:05:56.949 --> 00:06:00.099
And besides, he would end up with more each
week than he was getting with the old band.

00:06:00.099 --> 00:06:02.550
With the Tommy Dorsey Band, Frank travelled
across the country.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:04.389
He ended up in Hollywood, California.

00:06:04.389 --> 00:06:09.050
In 1942, the band, with Sinatra out fron,t
made a cameo in the MGM move ‘Ship Ahoy.’

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:12.360
With the band behind him Frank had built up
his profile.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:15.960
He appeared on the radio, in concert halls,
on TV and in the movies.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:20.400
By the end of 1942, he was one of the most
well known singers in the country – and

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.590
he had replaced Bing Crosby as Billboard’s
top band vocalist and cut his first record

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:26.650
with the band.

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:28.259
Going Solo

00:06:28.259 --> 00:06:34.120
By the end of 1942, Frank had made up his
mind to go it alone.

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:38.150
He was clearly the star attraction of the
Tommy Dorsey Band, so, while they may have

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:42.139
needed him, he was more than able to hold
an audience by himself.

00:06:42.139 --> 00:06:45.240
When he told Dorsey of his intentions, the
band leader was furious.

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:48.479
But Frank was determined and finally Dorsey
relented.

00:06:48.479 --> 00:06:51.990
He did insist, however, that the contract
for a third of Sinatra’s earnings would

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:53.409
remain in place.

00:06:53.409 --> 00:06:56.120
Frank decided that he would worry about the
contract later.

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:59.310
For now he would get out before Dorsey changed
his mind.

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:04.360
Sinatra made his solo debut on his 27th birthday,
December 12th, 1942.

00:07:04.360 --> 00:07:07.960
In the audience that night was Bob Weitman,
manager of the Paramount Theatre.

00:07:07.960 --> 00:07:12.409
He was so impressed that he asked Benny Goodman,
the King of Swing, if he minded having Frank

00:07:12.409 --> 00:07:13.979
on a bill with him at the Paramount.

00:07:13.979 --> 00:07:17.410
Goodman’s response was . . .
Who the hell is Frank Sinatra?

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:19.289
He would soon find out.

00:07:19.289 --> 00:07:21.370
Sinatra’s appearances at the Paramount made
history.

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:25.599
It was the start of the type of hysteria,
epitomized by screaming and fainting girls,

00:07:25.599 --> 00:07:28.590
that would later greet Elvis and the Beatles.

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:33.050
When Frank hit the stage, the theater erupted
with wild scenes that had never been seem

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:34.050
before.

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:39.030
In 1944, with his popularity soaring, Frank
became a father for a second time.

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:42.719
Frank Sinatra, junior joined older sister
Nancy at home while their father’s star

00:07:42.719 --> 00:07:43.720
kept ascending.

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:48.919
His record sales topped the billings and he
was the most requested voice on the radio.

00:07:48.919 --> 00:07:53.930
Losing It
With the end of World War Two, Sinatra’s

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:55.490
fame rose to yet another level.

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:59.340
He was contracted by Hollywood to star in
a series of musicals, each more popular than

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:00.340
the last.

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:03.110
Yet, in his personal life, things were starting
to unravel.

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:06.309
He was away from home so much that he hardly
saw his wife and children.

00:08:06.309 --> 00:08:11.469
Then, in 1947, Frank met and fell in love
with the beautiful and glamorous actress ava

00:08:11.469 --> 00:08:12.469
Gardner.

00:08:12.469 --> 00:08:16.000
The two began a very public affair, which
lost Sinatra quite a lot of popularity among

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:17.000
the older demographic.

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:21.590
At the same time, the 31-year-old of crooner’s
voice was beginning to show signs of strain

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:26.759
and he was also being attacked by mainstream
media outlets for his support of liberal causes.

00:08:26.759 --> 00:08:31.060
It was said that his fight against segregation
was Communist inspired.

00:08:31.060 --> 00:08:34.450
Reporters also began claiming that he dodged
the draft during the war.

00:08:34.450 --> 00:08:37.800
Leading the print assault on Sinatra was reporter
Lee Mortimer.

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:43.169
On April 8th, 1947, the two men came face
to face at Cirro’s Nightclub in Hollywood.

00:08:43.169 --> 00:08:46.589
Frank went straight up to the reporter, called
him a ‘fruit’ and then proceeded to use

00:08:46.589 --> 00:08:47.779
him as a punching bag.

00:08:47.779 --> 00:08:50.440
Frank’s bodyguards quickly broke it up,
with Sinatra yelling . . .

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:53.840
Next time I see you, I’ll kill you, you
little degenerate.

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:56.950
Mortimer had Frank arrested and charged with
assault and battery.

00:08:56.950 --> 00:08:59.620
The charge resulted in Sinatra’s gun permit
being revoked.

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:05.550
Mortimer later sued for $250,000, and the
case was eventually settled for $9,000.

00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:10.920
Unwise Associates
Sinatra never seemed to appreciate that his

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:13.910
choice of associates could impact his career.

00:09:13.910 --> 00:09:18.550
In 1947, not long after the beat up on Mortimer,
he went to Havana to meet with mobster boss

00:09:18.550 --> 00:09:19.660
Lucky Luciano.

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:23.269
The mob connection was to stick to him for
the rest of his life.

00:09:23.269 --> 00:09:27.680
Despite the affair with Ava Gardner, Nancy
Sinatra stuck by her husband’s side.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:31.410
She gave birth to a third child, Christina,
on Father’s Day, 1948.

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:34.260
But the new addition did not bring Frank back
into the family fold.

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:36.760
His fascination with Ava was greater than
ever.

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:40.210
Unlike Nancy, Ava could match him drink for
drink, cigarette for cigarette and tantrum

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:41.210
for tantrum.

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:43.680
They were the ideal hard living couple.

00:09:43.680 --> 00:09:45.540
That hard living had a price.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:47.389
In 1949, Frank’s voice gave out.

00:09:47.389 --> 00:09:51.920
That combined with public reaction to his
outrageous affair led to plummeting record

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:53.450
sales and chart numbers.

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:57.170
His radio show was dropped and MGM cancelled
his movie contract.

00:09:57.170 --> 00:10:00.590
It looked like the show was over for the man
who had been called The Voice.

00:10:00.590 --> 00:10:03.550
In 1951, Nancy finally divorced from Frank.

00:10:03.550 --> 00:10:05.480
In short order, Sinatra married Ava Gardner.

00:10:05.480 --> 00:10:09.800
He had formalized his love life, but it seemed
there was nothing he could do to resurrect

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:11.139
his failing career.

00:10:11.139 --> 00:10:16.300
The public had become disenchanted with him,
viewing him as a washed up has been.

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:21.180
Coming Back
It was during his period of hardship in the

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:26.630
early 1950’s that Sinatra read a book called
From Here to Eternity by James Jones.

00:10:26.630 --> 00:10:30.190
When he learned that a movie version was being
planned he became convinced that this could

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:32.130
be his road back to the top.

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:36.089
He set his sights on securing the role of
Private Angelo Maggio.

00:10:36.089 --> 00:10:40.139
Frank secured an audition and then proceeded
to convince the movie’s producers that he

00:10:40.139 --> 00:10:41.139
WAS Maggio.

00:10:41.139 --> 00:10:43.940
He won the part – and gave the performance
of his life.

00:10:43.940 --> 00:10:47.280
Sinatra won the Oscar for Best Supporting
Actor.

00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:50.560
The following month he signed a recording
contract with Capitol Records – and his

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:52.120
career was back on track.

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:57.100
It appeared that losing his fame and then
clawing back had changed Sinatra as a singer.

00:10:57.100 --> 00:11:01.389
He now wore his heart on his sleeve in his
performances, sharing his inner struggles

00:11:01.389 --> 00:11:02.389
with the audience.

00:11:02.389 --> 00:11:05.889
He became the first, and the best, musical
storyteller, and it was this ability that

00:11:05.889 --> 00:11:08.670
brought him the male audience that he had
never had before.

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:12.750
In Sinatra they saw the tender tough guy that
they wanted to be.

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:17.580
Sinatra’s marriage to Ava Gardner was tumultuous
and it ended in divorce in 1957 – but his

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:19.860
career was going fro9m strength to strength.

00:11:19.860 --> 00:11:23.040
By the late 1950’s, he was well and truly
back on top.

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:26.959
His Album Come Fly With Me hit Number One
and was on the charts for an impressive 71

00:11:26.959 --> 00:11:27.959
weeks.

00:11:27.959 --> 00:11:30.720
At the same time his movie career was hitting
new heights.

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.070
In The Man With Golden Arm he gave an extraordinary
performance as a heroin addicted misfit.

00:11:35.070 --> 00:11:39.800
The 1960’s saw the emergence of a new brand
of popular music – rock and roll.

00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:44.480
Realizing that he couldn’t ignore it, Sinatra
tried to accommodate by bringing out up tempo

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:45.480
albums.

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:49.500
In the early 60’s Sinatra also became a
staunch supporter of John F. Kennedy.

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:52.570
However, Frank had maintained his association
with key mob figures.

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:56.491
Attorney General Robert Kennedy was concerned
that Sinatra brought these criminal underworld

00:11:56.491 --> 00:11:58.310
figures too close to the President.

00:11:58.310 --> 00:12:02.050
He managed to sever the relationship between
JFK and Sinatra.

00:12:02.050 --> 00:12:06.850
Sinatra’s last great movie role was 1962’s
The Manchurian Candidate, in which he played

00:12:06.850 --> 00:12:08.930
US Army officer Bennet Marco.

00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:13.089
After that he appeared to become bored with
movies and developed a reputation as a troublemaker

00:12:13.089 --> 00:12:14.100
on the set.

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:17.819
His attention had switched to Las Vegas, the
new gambling capital of the nation.

00:12:17.819 --> 00:12:20.590
There he and his buddies became known as the
Rat Pack.

00:12:20.590 --> 00:12:24.900
Along with Dean Martin, Sammy Davis Junior,
Peter Lawford and Joey Bishop, Sinatra performed

00:12:24.900 --> 00:12:28.970
a mixture of stand-up comedy and lounge music
at the best casinos in town.

00:12:28.970 --> 00:12:34.110
Then, in December of 1963, Sinatra’s fun
loving existence was jarringly interrupted

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:40.250
when his 19-year-old son, Frank Junior, was
kidnapped and held for $240,000 ransom.

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:42.740
Sinatra appealed to Robert Kennedy, who called
in the FBI.

00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:47.060
The ransom was paid, Frank Junior released,
and the kidnappers promptly caught, with most

00:12:47.060 --> 00:12:48.630
of the ransom being returned.

00:12:48.630 --> 00:12:52.899
In 1964, while filming Von Ryan’s Express,
Sinatra noticed a young actress named Mia

00:12:52.899 --> 00:12:54.829
Farrow hanging around the set.

00:12:54.829 --> 00:12:59.180
The star of a popular night time soap opera,
Farrow, at just 19, soon became Sinatra’s

00:12:59.180 --> 00:13:00.360
constant companion.

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:05.070
The 30 year age gap provided plenty for the
gossip columnists to get their teeth into.

00:13:05.070 --> 00:13:09.310
In 1965, the 50-year-old singer won an Emmy
for the best Television Special of the Year.

00:13:09.310 --> 00:13:13.440
The following year he recorded one of his
most popular albums of all time, Strangers

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:14.540
in the Night.

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:17.779
That same year, 1966, Sinatra and Mia were
married.

00:13:17.779 --> 00:13:22.019
However, Mia’s mellow hippie style was out
of step with Frank’s hard living ways and

00:13:22.019 --> 00:13:24.899
the union only lasted sixteen months.

00:13:24.899 --> 00:13:28.430
Following the divorce and finding himself
in danger of being yesterday’s news, Sinatra

00:13:28.430 --> 00:13:32.730
began looking for a new musical direction
that would allow him to compete with the hard-edged

00:13:32.730 --> 00:13:34.950
rock and roll that was popular at the time.

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:38.269
He found what he was looking for in the form
of the song ‘My Way’, written by Paul

00:13:38.269 --> 00:13:39.269
Anka.

00:13:39.269 --> 00:13:42.850
The song, which Frank recorded in 1969, has
become his anthem.

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:47.529
In 1970, to the shock of his legion of fans,
Sinatra announced his retirement.

00:13:47.529 --> 00:13:52.019
He said that he was tired of show business
and wanted nothing more than to relax, read

00:13:52.019 --> 00:13:53.019
and think.

00:13:53.019 --> 00:13:56.699
But just as he prepared to take life easy,
life threw another curve ball his way.

00:13:56.699 --> 00:14:00.320
He was summoned to a congressional hearing
keen to investigate his connections with the

00:14:00.320 --> 00:14:01.320
mafia.

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:04.980
Lack of evidence forced the Congressional
committee to drop its investigation and the

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:06.329
heat went off Sinatra.

00:14:06.329 --> 00:14:10.920
Retirement never sat easily with Frank and,
by 1973, he was once again on the comeback

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:11.920
trail.

00:14:11.920 --> 00:14:14.410
This time his focus would be on concert performances.

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:18.339
He leapt back into the public consciousness
with a television special called ‘Ol’

00:14:18.339 --> 00:14:19.420
Blue Eyes Is Back.’

00:14:19.420 --> 00:14:23.449
From there he went on fill all of the concert
halls and big arenas across the country.

00:14:23.449 --> 00:14:28.449
He sang directly to his audience dressed in
a fitted tuxedo – just as he had always

00:14:28.449 --> 00:14:29.449
done.

00:14:29.449 --> 00:14:31.950
In 1976, the 60-year old Sinatra married for
the fourth time.

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:34.440
His bride was 45-year-old Barbara Marx.

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:37.410
She travelled with him as he played to ever
growing crowds.

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:41.019
By the 1980’s the Chairman of the Board
was a bona fide icon.

00:14:41.019 --> 00:14:43.890
He campaigned for Ronald Reagan, and helped
produce his second inaugural.

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:48.089
Then, in 1988, the 73-year-old paired with
some of his former Rat Pack performers.

00:14:48.089 --> 00:14:50.060
The audiences loved it.

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:55.010
In 1993, Frank made use of a technological
innovation to produce an album entitled ‘Frank

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:56.010
Sinatra Duets.’

00:14:56.010 --> 00:15:00.360
He sang along with 13 top recording artists,
though they were at different place and times.

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:05.129
The record became a number one platinum bestseller,
and outsold any of his previous recordings.

00:15:05.129 --> 00:15:06.129
After more than a century he was back on top.

00:15:06.129 --> 00:15:08.870
Sinatra lived out the last five years of his
life in relative quiet.

00:15:08.870 --> 00:15:12.680
He was plagued by ill health, often going
into hospital with cardiac complaints.

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:14.230
Then dementia set in.

00:15:14.230 --> 00:15:18.589
He passed away on May 14, 1998 at Cedar Sinai
hospital in Los Angeles with the official

00:15:18.589 --> 00:15:20.509
cause of death being heart attack.

00:15:20.509 --> 00:15:24.579
On May 15th, the Empire State building was
turned blue in New York and the casinos in

00:15:24.579 --> 00:16:02.269
Las Vegas came to a halt in tribute to the
man who, very uniquely, did it his way.

